data.content:
Here are the improved MSTest assertions for your collection tests:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));  // All() is fine here
    var admin = users.Single(u => u.Role == "Admin");
    Assert.IsNotNull(admin);
}
```

**Key replacements:**

| Instead of | Use |
|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count > 0)` | `Assert.IsTrue(users.Any())` or `Assert.AreEqual(expected, users.Count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count() == 3)` | `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(obj != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(obj)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(users.All(...))` | Already fine — no dedicated `CollectionAssert` equivalent for predicates |

**Also available via `CollectionAssert`:**

```csharp
CollectionAssert.AllAreSatisfied(users, u => Assert.IsTrue(u.IsActive));  // MSTest v3+
CollectionAssert.Contains(users, expectedUser);
CollectionAssert.AreEquivalent(expected, actual);  // same items, any order
```

The main principle: use the most specific assertion so failure messages tell you *what* went wrong (e.g., "Expected 3, got 2") instead of just "Assert.IsTrue failed."
